※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=85 diag=none

※ A program that asks for more memory than the system will give stops with a status of its own
※ and says where, rather than being killed.  The heap asks the system for room and the system's
※ refusal is what stops it -- so this test is also what says the suite runs every program under a
※ limit (`tests/limits.py`): without one, doubling a string forever would take the machine.

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    let piece: mut str = "0123456789abcdef"
    while true:
        piece ← piece ⧺ piece
    0u6
